---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const headings = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'what-it-fixes', label: 'What it fixes' },
  { id: 'usage', label: 'Rendering and hydrating' },
  { id: 'fouc', label: 'The FOUC guard' },
  { id: 'coverage', label: 'Component coverage' },
  { id: 'frameworks', label: 'Framework support' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Server Rendering"
  description="Server-render ARC UI components to declarative shadow DOM, and hydrate them without a flash."
  activeSlug="server-rendering"
  headings={headings}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Server Rendering' }]}
>
  <div class="prose-page">
    <h1>Server Rendering</h1>

    <section id="overview">
      <p>
        ARC UI components server-render through <code>@lit-labs/ssr</code> into
        <strong>declarative shadow DOM</strong> — a <code>&lt;template shadowrootmode="open"&gt;</code>
        the browser turns into a real shadow root while parsing, before any JavaScript runs.
        The component's content is in the initial HTML response.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>185 of 186 components</strong> render this way today. This is optional: a consumer
        with no build step, or loading ARC from a CDN, needs none of it and loses nothing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="what-it-fixes">
      <h2>What it fixes</h2>
      <p>
        Most ARC components take their content from properties rather than slots. With JavaScript
        disabled, or before it loads, this is an empty unknown tag — no heading, no description,
        nothing for a crawler to read:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`<arc-feature-card
  heading="Fast by default"
  description="Ships 40% less JavaScript"
></arc-feature-card>`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <strong>65 of 185 components</strong> are shaped like this — they have no default slot at
        all, so there is no markup to fall back to. Server rendering is the only thing that puts
        their content in the payload. Slot-driven components already degrade gracefully, and gain
        less from it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="usage">
      <h2>Rendering and hydrating</h2>
      <p>On the server, render as you would any Lit template:</p>
      <pre><code>{`import { render } from '@lit-labs/ssr';
import { collectResult } from '@lit-labs/ssr/lib/render-result.js';
import { html } from 'lit';
import '@arclux/arc-ui/feature-card';

const markup = await collectResult(render(html\`
  <arc-feature-card heading="Fast by default"></arc-feature-card>
\`));`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        On the client, import the hydration entry <strong>before any component</strong>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`import '@arclux/arc-ui/hydrate';   // first — order matters
import '@arclux/arc-ui/register';`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Without it, the moment an element upgrades Lit renders its template from scratch into the
        shadow root the server already filled — discarding identical DOM and producing the exact
        flash server rendering exists to avoid. With it, Lit <em>adopts</em> the existing nodes and
        renders nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Import order matters because hydration support patches <code>LitElement</code>'s update
        path. A component class defined before the patch never receives it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="fouc">
      <h2>The FOUC guard</h2>
      <p>
        <code>base.css</code> hides ARC elements until they upgrade, so a page never flashes
        unstyled custom elements. A server-rendered element is un-upgraded but <em>finished</em>,
        so that rule would hide completed content for the whole hydration window — turning server
        rendering into a longer blank screen rather than a shorter one.
      </p>
      <p>Opt the page out at the root:</p>
      <pre><code>{`<html data-arc-ssr>`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        This is a root-level switch rather than something automatic because CSS cannot detect a
        declarative shadow root: the browser consumes the <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> during
        parsing and leaves no selectable trace of it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="coverage">
      <h2>Component coverage</h2>
      <p>
        <code>pnpm check:ssr</code> renders every component in Node and fails on any that throws;
        it runs in CI. Server-side, Lit runs the constructor, <code>willUpdate</code> and
        <code>render</code> — and none of <code>connectedCallback</code>,
        <code>firstUpdated</code>, <code>updated</code>, or a reactive controller's
        <code>hostConnected</code>. Browser work belongs in those.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>185 components</strong> server-render, all emitting declarative shadow DOM.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>arc-markdown</code> is client-only by design.</strong> Its sanitiser needs
          <code>DOMParser</code>. Rendering unsanitised HTML on the server is not an option, and
          rendering different markup would break hydration, which reconnects to the server's DOM
          rather than re-rendering it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Support is a property of the library, not of individual components — there is no
        per-component opt-in to track, because nearly everything works. You opt in per page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="frameworks">
      <h2>Framework support</h2>
      <p>
        Declarative shadow DOM has to be produced by Lit's server renderer. A framework's own SSR
        emits <code>&lt;arc-feature-card&gt;</code> as an unknown tag and knows nothing about its
        shadow content, so the framework needs an integration to call into Lit SSR.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Plain HTML, Lit, or any server that renders templates</strong> — fully supported
          today, as above.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>React</strong> — <code>@lit-labs/ssr-react</code> exists for this and is the
          supported path.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vue, Svelte, Solid, Angular, Preact</strong> — no Lit SSR integration exists.
          Their SSR output contains the element tag without its shadow content, and the component
          renders on the client at upgrade. Everything still works; the content simply is not in
          the initial payload.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Hydration support and the FOUC opt-out apply to every one of these, since both are
        properties of the element rather than of the framework around it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

